.eth1.dhcp), and node2/node3 diffs show only expected reboot-generated systemd-private directories, not residue from experimentation.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7a588164828d4c7d","cmd-cd65be3ef8e24c46","cmd-55fd24f6e6c445c4","cmd-9065d43165d0477a","cmd-92e3be446a98420e","cmd-dea9fc74baca4d03","cmd-79d052bd404b45e7","cmd-3f89c35d55a14589","cmd-2349c168eda243d1","cmd-803a6c5431944a42","cmd-7cac99bfd7374643","cmd-d013ffcfab964311","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All three material outcomes of the task — node1 acting as DHCP server on the shared private network, node2 receiving a fixed address across repeated leases, and node3 drawing from the general pool — are directly and repeatedly demonstrated, including consistency after each node's independent restart. The mandatory restart-evidence protocol was also completed cleanly for all three nodes with clear pre/post boot-identity changes and confirmed recovery of relevant service and networking state. No unnecessary or unrestored mutations were found in the command trace.","limitations":["Since after_prepare is an alias of before_prepare (prepare disabled), the executor-caused-vs-preexisting distinction relies solely on the before_prepare vs after_executor diff, which is adequate here but leaves no independent preparer baseline.","node1's post-restart boot-identity observation in the fixed after_executor snapshot returned a connection error (return_code 255) rather than a captured value, so the automated cross-check for node1 relies on the executor's own timeline evidence (cmd-cd56cedee5674859) rather than the fixed snapshot.","Firewall rule effectiveness was inferred from UCI zone membership rather than an explicit packet-level test of DHCP/firewall traffic beyond the successful lease acquisitions and pings, though those successful leases and pings are themselves strong indirect confirmation."]},"ttft